    "note": "Value is the +/-2% strain-window fit (5.4673 A, all 3 EOS forms within 0.0001 A). The mean over all 27 fits is 5.4665 A, range 5.4653-5.4673 A. Wider windows shift a0 down by up to 0.002 A because of EOS-form bias. Cell size has no effect (<1e-5 A). The uncertainty of 0.002 A covers ONLY the numerical/fit spread (EOS form, strain window, cell size). It does NOT cover model error. PREMISE: well-posed. A static EOS gives the 0 K classical lattice constant of a perfect crystal. Si is in the training domain of MACE-MPA-0, which is trained on PBE data (MPtrj+sAlex). So the number targets PBE, not experiment. VALIDATION (same model, same protocol): fcc Cu/Al/Ag give 3.639/4.038/4.162 A. That matches the PBE values 3.642/4.042/4.147 A tabulated in arXiv:2604.07046 (Table C.1, LC20 set) to within -0.1%/-0.1%/+0.4%. The same table gives experimental reference values of 3.595/4.018/4.062 A, so MACE is +1.2%/+0.5%/+2.4% vs experiment, the known PBE overestimate. Also computed, but with no sourced reference on hand: Ge 5.720 A (PBE is known to strongly overestimate Ge), diamond C 3.578 A, B(Si) = 87.6 GPa. COMPARISON: CODATA 2018/2022 Si lattice parameter is 5.431020511(89) A (vacuum, 22.5 C; NIST physics.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/xray_2018.pdf). This result is +0.036 A (+0.66%) above it. That is consistent with the PBE-type bias seen in the validation set. Zero-point and thermal-expansion effects (0 K static vs 295 K) are much smaller than this offset in Si and were not computed. Realistic error vs experiment is therefore about +0.5 to +1% (+0.03 to +0.05 A), dominated by the PBE reference of the model."
